course: dictionary
lesson: 10915
title: The vocative: calling someone
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: When you speak straight to someone, you often use their name or a word like Mum. That word is called the vocative. In «Tom, come here!», the word Tom tells us who is being called. The comma sets it apart from the rest of the sentence.
explain: The comma matters. «Let's eat, Grandma!» invites Grandma to dinner, but «Let's eat Grandma!» is a very different, very silly sentence. A comma around the vocative shows who is being spoken to.
explain: In some languages, such as Latin, Ukrainian and Polish, the vocative is a special form. The word changes its ending when you call someone. Ukrainian «Ivan» becomes «Ivane» when you call him.
fact: The vocative is the word or name used to speak directly to someone.
fact: In English, set off a vocative with a comma: «Thank you, Mum.»
fact: In Ukrainian, the vocative is the case for calling someone, so you call your mum «Мамо!».
fact: The vocative changes the ending of a name: «Mama» becomes «Mamo!» and «Tato» becomes «Tatu!».
fact: Old English had a calling form too, but it was lost over time.
exercise: In «Tom, come here!», which word is the vocative? | come | here | *Tom
exercise: What does the comma in «Let's eat, Grandma!» show? | That the sentence is finished, so stop reading | That it is a question | *That Grandma is the one being spoken to
exercise: In Ukrainian, what is the calling form of Taras? | *Тарасе! | Тарас | Тарасу
test: In «Thank you, Mum.», which word is the vocative? | *Mum | you | Thank
test: What does the vocative do in languages like Ukrainian? | It always keeps the name exactly the same | *It changes the ending of the name when you call someone | It adds a comma to the end of every word | It makes the name longer in every sentence
exam: What is a vocative? | *The word or name used to speak directly to someone | A question word such as who or where | A word that joins two sentences | A verb that tells you what to do
